WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.848
Make process-launching code easy to find.

2
00:00:02.928 --> 00:00:07.101
Review direct uses of exec, execFile,
spawn, fork, shell-enabled options,

3
00:00:07.181 --> 00:00:10.051
wrapper libraries, and internal helper
functions.

4
00:00:10.131 --> 00:00:13.913
Search for command strings,
request-controlled executable names,

5
00:00:13.993 --> 00:00:17.254
arbitrary argument forwarding, inherited
environments,

6
00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:19.225
and dynamic working directories.

7
00:00:19.305 --> 00:00:22.500
A wrapper with a reassuring name is not
evidence of safety;

8
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.558
reviewers need to trace the final
executable, argument array, options,

9
00:00:26.638 --> 00:00:29.442
and data provenance at the actual launch
boundary.

10
00:00:29.522 --> 00:00:32.131
Review each launch as a capability
definition.

11
00:00:32.211 --> 00:00:35.406
Confirm that a shell is absent unless its
use is justified,

12
00:00:35.486 --> 00:00:38.095
the executable comes from a closed trusted
map,

13
00:00:38.175 --> 00:00:41.305
and structural arguments are fixed by
application code.

14
00:00:41.385 --> 00:00:45.363
Identify every variable operand and its
validation and authorization.

15
00:00:45.443 --> 00:00:49.617
Examine whether the target treats a value
as an option, configuration path,

16
00:00:49.697 --> 00:00:52.957
plugin, script, output location, or
network destination.

17
00:00:53.037 --> 00:00:56.950
Then verify path mapping, environment
minimization, controlled cwd,

18
00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.356
child privilege, timeouts, concurrency,
output handling,

19
00:01:00.436 --> 00:01:02.327
and client-safe error reporting.

20
00:01:02.407 --> 00:01:06.711
Tests should verify process structure
instead of collecting attack strings.

21
00:01:06.791 --> 00:01:11.160
Exercise malformed values, unexpected
punctuation, option-prefixed names,

22
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.805
oversized work items, invalid storage
identifiers, unsupported operation keys,

23
00:01:15.885 --> 00:01:17.777
and request attempts to add flags.

24
00:01:17.857 --> 00:01:20.987
Assert the exact executable, argument
boundaries, cwd,

25
00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:24.849
and selected environment passed to a fake
or instrumented launcher.

26
00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:28.972
Integration tests should cover child
failure, nonzero exits, timeouts,

27
00:01:29.052 --> 00:01:32.900
cancellation, large stdout and stderr,
cleanup, repeated requests,

28
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:34.349
and concurrency limits.

29
00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:38.407
Run platform-specific cases wherever the
application claims support.

30
00:01:38.487 --> 00:01:42.465
Keep diagnostics useful without exposing
secrets, full environments,

31
00:01:42.545 --> 00:01:45.806
sensitive paths, or untrusted output
directly to clients.

32
00:01:45.886 --> 00:01:50.516
Record operation identifiers, duration,
exit category, bounded output metadata,

33
00:01:50.596 --> 00:01:53.205
and cancellation outcome according to
policy.

34
00:01:53.285 --> 00:01:57.458
Do not place raw child output into logs
that downstream tools may interpret,

35
00:01:57.538 --> 00:02:01.907
and never return implementation details as
a substitute for stable API errors.

36
00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:06.291
A narrow launcher interface plus targeted
tests and telemetry makes security

37
00:02:06.371 --> 00:02:08.980
behavior reviewable throughout
refactoring.
